
part A 
A GitHub repository or repo is a digital container that holds all project files code images and documentation along with a full history of every change made. People create repositories to store code safely in the cloud work together without losing changes and keep project tasks organized.
To make a repository you log into GitHub select the plus icon to choose New repository type in a name choose if it should be public or private and click Create repository.
After making a repo you will find several helpful tabs across the top. The Code tab shows all project files and the main explanation page. The Issues tab keeps track of bugs tasks and requested changes. The Pull Requests tab lets team members review and combine code additions. The Actions tab runs automated tests to make sure code works. The Projects tab provides task boards to keep work on schedule. The Wiki tab holds longer instructions for users. The Security tab checks for safety risks in the code. The Insights tab shows graphs of project activity. Finally the Settings tab lets you control access and change repo options.
part B
Focused mode is a state of intense concentration where your brain pays close attention to specific details and uses established thought patterns to analyze information. Diffuse mode is a relaxed, big-picture state where your mind wanders freely, allowing your brain to subconsciously connect different ideas and form new creative insights.  
Learnable
The main difference between the two is that focused mode operates through direct conscious effort on specific tasks, while diffuse mode operates passively in the background when you step away and relax. Furthermore, you cannot be in both modes at the same time.  
Learnable
Both modes are essential for learning difficult subjects because focused mode allows you to understand the precise details and mechanics of a problem, while diffuse mode gives your brain the spatial freedom to make unexpected neural connections, grasp complex overall concepts, and break through mental blocks. Cycling between intense focus and relaxed breaks is the most effective way to truly master challenging material.
